{"revision_id":105,"metadata":{"citation_count":150,"publication_info":[{"year":2011,"artid":"038","page_start":"038","journal_title":"JCAP","journal_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1212538"},"journal_volume":"11"}],"citation_count_without_self_citations":140,"core":true,"dois":[{"value":"10.1088/1475-7516/2011/11/038"}],"titles":[{"title":"The (not so) squeezed limit of the primordial 3-point function"},{"title":"The (not so) squeezed limit of the primordial 3-point function","source":"arXiv"}],"$schema":"https://inspirehep.net/schemas/records/hep.json","authors":[{"uuid":"f63f8f6c-b9e2-4e1c-afd0-18cc5705a722","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1012813"},"full_name":"Creminelli, Paolo","affiliations":[{"value":"ICTP, Trieste","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/902861"}}],"signature_block":"CRANANALp","curated_relation":true},{"uuid":"b0d90ad6-c545-4aa6-9d05-b83ebc92f959","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1043960"},"full_name":"D'Amico, Guido","affiliations":[{"value":"New York U., CCPP","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/909498"}}],"signature_block":"ANACg","curated_relation":true},{"uuid":"24f99f91-9622-4ff3-98ab-fe5554ad55b5","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1041867"},"full_name":"Musso, Marcello","affiliations":[{"value":"ICTP, Trieste","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/902861"}}],"signature_block":"MASm","curated_relation":true},{"uuid":"b843323d-c994-485f-9c5a-655ab08be01c","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1056459"},"full_name":"Norena, Jorge","affiliations":[{"value":"ICC, Barcelona U.","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/911212"}}],"signature_block":"NARANj","curated_relation":true}],"curated":true,"figures":[{"key":"1a6b688d960bf3f7543735bf7dff9b39","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/1a6b688d960bf3f7543735bf7dff9b39","source":"arxiv","caption":"\\small Expression \\eqref{eq:bias} for the modification to the halo bias in the presence of local non-Gaussianity multiplied by $k^2$ before integration over $k_1$, for a smoothing length of $R = 2\\,h^{-1}\\,\\mathrm{Mpc}$ at redshift $z = 1$.","filename":"integrand.png"},{"key":"786459170a0f7c950fae0e052b8dbefb","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/786459170a0f7c950fae0e052b8dbefb","source":"arxiv","caption":"\\small Comparison of the non-Gaussian correction from the local template to the halo bias from a ``modified'' local template with the extreme squeezed limit removed (see text). The blue solid line is a ``conservative'' estimate for a smoothing scale corresponding to $2.5\\times 10^{12} M_\\odot\\, h^{-1}$, the red dashed line is an ``optimistic'' estimate for larger scales and smaller objects with a mass of $3.1\\times 10^{11} M_\\odot\\,h^{-1}$. The choice of scale for the ``optimistic'' case corresponds roughly to the largest scale accessible to the planned EUCLID survey.","filename":"bias_alpha.png"}],"license":[{"url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","imposing":"arXiv"}],"texkeys":["Creminelli:2011rh"],"citeable":true,"refereed":true,"abstracts":[{"value":"We prove that, in a generic single-field model, the consistency relation for the 3-point function in the squeezed limit receives corrections that vanish quadratically in the ratio of the momenta, i.e. as (k_L/k_S)^2. This implies that a detection of a bispectrum signal going as 1/k_L^2 in the squeezed limit, that is suppressed only by one power of k_L compared with the local shape, would rule out all single-field models. The absence of this kind of terms in the bispectrum holds also for multifield models, but only if all the fields have a mass much smaller than H. The detection of any scale dependence of the bias, for scales much larger than the size of the haloes, would disprove all single-field models. We comment on the regime of squeezing that can be probed by realistic surveys.","source":"arXiv"}],"ref